(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-resources",{"k8s-resources/requests-limits-and-qos-classes":{"html":"\u003cp>Every container can declare how much CPU and memory it needs and how much it may use. Those two numbers are read by different components for different purposes, and getting them wrong is the most common cause of both wasted money and unexpected restarts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between a request and a limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How the scheduler uses requests and the kernel uses limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU units and memory units\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>QoS classes and their eviction order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to set sensible values\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"request-and-limit\">Request and limit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A request is the amount the container is guaranteed and what the scheduler reserves when it places the pod. A limit is the most it may use. CPU is measured in cores, with \u003ccode>500m\u003c/code> meaning half a core. Memory is in bytes, written as \u003ccode>256Mi\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>1Gi\u003c/code>. Requests above what any node can offer make the pod unschedulable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-enforces-what\">Who enforces what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The scheduler sums requests of pods on a node and compares them to the node allocatable resources. It never looks at actual usage. At runtime the kernel enforces limits through cgroups. CPU is compressible: a container at its CPU limit is throttled, not killed. Memory is not: a container that exceeds its memory limit is killed by the out-of-memory killer and restarted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"qos-classes\">QoS classes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet assigns every pod a quality of service class from its requests and limits. Guaranteed means every container has both CPU and memory, with requests equal to limits. Burstable means at least one request or limit is set but the pod is not Guaranteed. BestEffort means nothing is set. Under node memory pressure, the kubelet evicts BestEffort pods first, then Burstable pods using more than their requests, and Guaranteed pods last.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}{&quot;\\n&quot;}'\nkubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n '/Allocated resources/,/Events/p'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.qosClass}{&quot;\\n&quot;}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"setting-values\">Setting values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start from measurement. Look at real usage over a representative period, and set the request near typical or percentile usage and the memory limit with headroom above the peak. Many teams set memory request equal to limit for predictability and leave CPU limits unset or generous to avoid throttling. Latency sensitive and critical pods suit Guaranteed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: 250m\n    memory: 256Mi\n  limits:\n    memory: 512Mi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">250m\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-quick-calculation\">A quick calculation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"node_alloc_cpu = 3.8\nreq = {&quot;web&quot;: 0.25, &quot;api&quot;: 0.5, &quot;cache&quot;: 1.0}\nreplicas = {&quot;web&quot;: 4, &quot;api&quot;: 3, &quot;cache&quot;: 1}\nused = sum(req[k] * replicas[k] for k in req)\nprint(&quot;requested:&quot;, used, &quot;of&quot;, node_alloc_cpu, &quot;free:&quot;, round(node_alloc_cpu - used, 2))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">node_alloc_cpu = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.8\u003c/span>\nreq = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.25\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cache&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.0\u003c/span>}\nreplicas = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cache&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>}\nused = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(req[k] * replicas[k] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> req)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;requested:&quot;\u003c/span>, used, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;of&quot;\u003c/span>, node_alloc_cpu, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;free:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(node_alloc_cpu - used, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"spec&gt;sch;spec&gt;cg;cg&gt;qos\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Requests, limits and QoS classes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container spec\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sch\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cgroups\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"qos\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">QoS class\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Container spec to Scheduler; 2, Container spec to cgroups; 3, cgroups to QoS class.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spec\">\u003cdt>Container spec \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests and limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing values.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sch\">\u003cdt>Scheduler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reserves requests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overcommitted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cg\">\u003cdt>cgroups \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Enforce limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Throttle or OOM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"qos\">\u003cdt>QoS class \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sets eviction order.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> BestEffort.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-oomkilled-with-plenty-of-node-memory\">Incident: OOMKilled with plenty of node memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pod restarted every few hours while the node showed large free memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod api-5c7d | grep -A4 &quot;Last State&quot;\nkubectl get pod api-5c7d -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod api-5c7d | grep -A4 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Last State&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Last State:     Terminated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Reason:       OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Exit Code:    137\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod api-5c7d -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">256Mi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The memory limit applies per container, not per node. The application grew beyond 256 MiB during a batch job and the kernel killed it at its cgroup limit, regardless of free node memory. Raising the limit to match measured peaks plus headroom stopped the restarts, and a graph of container memory against its limit was added. Exit code 137 with OOMKilled is the signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting no requests at all\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting limits far above requests everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting memory limits below real peak usage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting CPU limits to kill containers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring QoS when a node is under pressure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"request-and-limit","text":"Request and limit"},{"id":"who-enforces-what","text":"Who enforces what"},{"id":"qos-classes","text":"QoS classes"},{"id":"setting-values","text":"Setting values"},{"id":"a-quick-calculation","text":"A quick calculation"},{"id":"incident-oomkilled-with-plenty-of-node-memory","text":"Incident: OOMKilled with plenty of node memory"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Resource management for pods and containers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/"},{"t":"Pod quality of service classes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/"}],"text":"Every container can declare how much CPU and memory it needs and how much it may use. Those two numbers are read by different components for different purposes, and getting them wrong is the most common cause of both wasted money and unexpected restarts. What you will learn The difference between a request and a limit How the scheduler uses requests and the kernel uses limits CPU units and memory units QoS classes and their eviction order How to set sensible values Request and limit A request is the amount the container is guaranteed and what the scheduler reserves when it places the pod. A limit is the most it may use. CPU is measured in cores, with 500m meaning half a core. Memory is in bytes, written as 256Mi or 1Gi . Requests above what any node can offer make the pod unschedulable. Who enforces what The scheduler sums requests of pods on a node and compares them to the node allocatable resources. It never looks at actual usage. At runtime the kernel enforces limits through cgroups. CPU is compressible: a container at its CPU limit is throttled, not killed. Memory is not: a container that exceeds its memory limit is killed by the out-of-memory killer and restarted. QoS classes The kubelet assigns every pod a quality of service class from its requests and limits. Guaranteed means every container has both CPU and memory, with requests equal to limits. Burstable means at least one request or limit is set but the pod is not Guaranteed. BestEffort means nothing is set. Under node memory pressure, the kubelet evicts BestEffort pods first, then Burstable pods using more than their requests, and Guaranteed pods last. kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.qosClass}{\"\\n\"}&#x27; kubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n &#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27; Setting values Start from measurement. Look at real usage over a representative period, and set the request near typical or percentile usage and the memory limit with headroom above the peak. Many teams set memory request equal to limit for predictability and leave CPU limits unset or generous to avoid throttling. Latency sensitive and critical pods suit Guaranteed. resources: requests: cpu: 250m memory: 256Mi limits: memory: 512Mi A quick calculation node_alloc_cpu = 3.8 req = { \"web\" : 0.25 , \"api\" : 0.5 , \"cache\" : 1.0 } replicas = { \"web\" : 4 , \"api\" : 3 , \"cache\" : 1 } used = sum (req[k] * replicas[k] for k in req) print ( \"requested:\" , used, \"of\" , node_alloc_cpu, \"free:\" , round (node_alloc_cpu - used, 2 )) Requests, limits and QoS classes Flow in order: 1, Container spec to Scheduler; 2, Container spec to cgroups; 3, cgroups to QoS class. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Container spec Data store What it does: Requests and limits. If it fails or is weak: Missing values. Scheduler Step What it does: Reserves requests. If it fails or is weak: Overcommitted. cgroups Step What it does: Enforce limits. If it fails or is weak: Throttle or OOM. QoS class Observability What it does: Sets eviction order. If it fails or is weak: BestEffort. Incident: OOMKilled with plenty of node memory Incident A pod restarted every few hours while the node showed large free memory. $ kubectl describe pod api-5c7d | grep -A4 \"Last State\" Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled Exit Code: 137 $ kubectl get pod api-5c7d -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27; 256Mi (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The memory limit applies per container, not per node. The application grew beyond 256 MiB during a batch job and the kernel killed it at its cgroup limit, regardless of free node memory. Raising the limit to match measured peaks plus headroom stopped the restarts, and a graph of container memory against its limit was added. Exit code 137 with OOMKilled is the signature. Common mistakes Setting no requests at all Setting limits "},"k8s-resources/cpu-and-memory-behaviour-throttling-and-oom":{"html":"\u003cp>Resource settings turn into kernel settings, and the kernel reacts in specific ways. Learning those reactions lets you tell throttling from a slow dependency and an OOM kill from an application crash.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How CPU requests become shares and limits become quota\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What throttling looks like and how to measure it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Working set memory and what counts toward the limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The OOM killer, oom_score_adj and exit code 137\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Practical tuning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cpu-shares-and-quota\">CPU shares and quota\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A CPU request sets cgroup cpu shares, a relative weight used when the node is busy. A CPU limit sets a CFS quota, for example 50 ms of CPU time in each 100 ms period for a limit of 0.5 cores. A multi-threaded application can use its whole quota in a few milliseconds and then be paused for the rest of the period, which shows up as latency spikes even when average CPU looks low.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-throttling\">Measuring throttling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The metric \u003ccode>container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total\u003c/code> against \u003ccode>container_cpu_cfs_periods_total\u003c/code> gives the throttled share. A pod that is throttled in a large share of periods needs a higher limit, or no limit, or fewer threads. Many teams drop CPU limits for latency sensitive workloads and rely on requests for fairness, while keeping namespace level quotas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pod -n shop\nkubectl exec api-1 -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl top pod -n shop\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> api-1 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory\">Memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The limit counts the working set: anonymous memory, page cache that cannot be dropped cheaply and kernel memory attributed to the container. Page cache that can be reclaimed does not cause a kill by itself. \u003ccode>kubectl top\u003c/code> shows the working set, which is what the kubelet uses for eviction decisions. Languages with managed heaps need explicit limits that respect the container, such as a Java heap percentage or Go memory limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-oom-killer\">The OOM killer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a cgroup hits its limit and reclaim fails, the kernel kills a process in it. Kubernetes sets oom_score_adj so that BestEffort processes are chosen first on node pressure and Guaranteed last. A kill shows as exit code 137 and reason OOMKilled. If the main process is killed, the container restarts with backoff. A killed child process may not be visible at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tuning-in-practice\">Tuning in practice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch usage and throttling over a week that includes peaks. Increase memory limits to cover the peak with margin and fix leaks rather than raising them endlessly. Choose CPU limits deliberately: leave them off for latency critical services or set them well above the request. Tune the runtime too, for example worker counts, to match the CPU you actually get.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"req&gt;cg;lim&gt;cg;cg&gt;thr;cg&gt;oom\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">CPU and memory behaviour: throttling and out-of-memory kills\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CPU request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lim\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CPU limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cgroup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"thr\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Throttling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"oom\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OOM kill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, CPU request to cgroup; 2, CPU limit to cgroup; 3, cgroup to Throttling; 4, cgroup to OOM kill.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>CPU request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Relative share.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too low.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lim\">\u003cdt>CPU limit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Quota per period.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Throttled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cg\">\u003cdt>cgroup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies both.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Misread.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"thr\">\u003cdt>Throttling \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Paused time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Latency spikes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"oom\">\u003cdt>OOM kill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exit 137.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restart loop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-latency-spikes-with-low-average-cpu\">Incident: Latency spikes with low average CPU\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service with a CPU limit of one core had periodic latency spikes while dashboards showed 30 percent CPU use.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec api-1 -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> api-1 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">usage_usec 91240000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nr_periods 18200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nr_throttled 6100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">throttled_usec 41200000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A third of all periods were throttled. The application ran eight worker threads that burned the quota early in each period and then waited. Raising the limit and reducing the worker count to match removed the spikes. Average CPU hides throttling, so look at the throttled share directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading average CPU as proof there is no throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting tiny CPU limits on multi-threaded services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raising memory limits to hide leaks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring runtime tuning inside the container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating page cache as application memory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cpu-shares-and-quota","text":"CPU shares and quota"},{"id":"measuring-throttling","text":"Measuring throttling"},{"id":"memory","text":"Memory"},{"id":"the-oom-killer","text":"The OOM killer"},{"id":"tuning-in-practice","text":"Tuning in practice"},{"id":"incident-latency-spikes-with-low-average-cpu","text":"Incident: Latency spikes with low average CPU"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Resource management for pods and containers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/"},{"t":"Linux CFS bandwidth control","u":"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/scheduler/sched-bwc.html"}],"text":"Resource settings turn into kernel settings, and the kernel reacts in specific ways. Learning those reactions lets you tell throttling from a slow dependency and an OOM kill from an application crash. What you will learn How CPU requests become shares and limits become quota What throttling looks like and how to measure it Working set memory and what counts toward the limit The OOM killer, oom_score_adj and exit code 137 Practical tuning CPU shares and quota A CPU request sets cgroup cpu shares, a relative weight used when the node is busy. A CPU limit sets a CFS quota, for example 50 ms of CPU time in each 100 ms period for a limit of 0.5 cores. A multi-threaded application can use its whole quota in a few milliseconds and then be paused for the rest of the period, which shows up as latency spikes even when average CPU looks low. Measuring throttling The metric container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total against container_cpu_cfs_periods_total gives the throttled share. A pod that is throttled in a large share of periods needs a higher limit, or no limit, or fewer threads. Many teams drop CPU limits for latency sensitive workloads and rely on requests for fairness, while keeping namespace level quotas. kubectl top pod -n shop kubectl exec api-1 -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat Memory The limit counts the working set: anonymous memory, page cache that cannot be dropped cheaply and kernel memory attributed to the container. Page cache that can be reclaimed does not cause a kill by itself. kubectl top shows the working set, which is what the kubelet uses for eviction decisions. Languages with managed heaps need explicit limits that respect the container, such as a Java heap percentage or Go memory limit. The OOM killer When a cgroup hits its limit and reclaim fails, the kernel kills a process in it. Kubernetes sets oom_score_adj so that BestEffort processes are chosen first on node pressure and Guaranteed last. A kill shows as exit code 137 and reason OOMKilled. If the main process is killed, the container restarts with backoff. A killed child process may not be visible at all. Tuning in practice Watch usage and throttling over a week that includes peaks. Increase memory limits to cover the peak with margin and fix leaks rather than raising them endlessly. Choose CPU limits deliberately: leave them off for latency critical services or set them well above the request. Tune the runtime too, for example worker counts, to match the CPU you actually get. CPU and memory behaviour: throttling and out-of-memory kills Flow in order: 1, CPU request to cgroup; 2, CPU limit to cgroup; 3, cgroup to Throttling; 4, cgroup to OOM kill. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. CPU request Data store What it does: Relative share. If it fails or is weak: Too low. CPU limit Data store What it does: Quota per period. If it fails or is weak: Throttled. cgroup Step What it does: Applies both. If it fails or is weak: Misread. Throttling Observability What it does: Paused time. If it fails or is weak: Latency spikes. OOM kill Observability What it does: Exit 137. If it fails or is weak: Restart loop. Incident: Latency spikes with low average CPU Incident A service with a CPU limit of one core had periodic latency spikes while dashboards showed 30 percent CPU use. $ kubectl exec api-1 -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat usage_usec 91240000 nr_periods 18200 nr_throttled 6100 throttled_usec 41200000 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A third of all periods were throttled. The application ran eight worker threads that burned the quota early in each period and then waited. Raising the limit and reducing the worker count to match removed the spikes. Average CPU hides throttling, so look at the throttled share directly. Common mistakes Reading average CPU as proof there is no throttling Setting tiny CPU limits on multi-threaded services Raising me"},"k8s-resources/limitrange-resourcequota-and-priority":{"html":"\u003cp>Without guardrails, one team can request all the cluster. Namespace level objects set defaults and ceilings, and priorities say which workloads matter when there is not enough for everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>LimitRange defaults, minimums and maximums\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ResourceQuota for compute, storage and object counts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What happens when a quota blocks a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>PriorityClass and preemption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A model for sharing a cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limitrange\">LimitRange\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A LimitRange in a namespace supplies default requests and limits for containers that omit them and enforces minimum and maximum values per container or pod. Defaults mean no pod is BestEffort by accident, and maximums stop a single container from asking for an entire node.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: LimitRange\nmetadata:\n  name: defaults\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  limits:\n    - type: Container\n      default:\n        memory: 512Mi\n      defaultRequest:\n        cpu: 100m\n        memory: 256Mi\n      max:\n        memory: 2Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LimitRange\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">defaults\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Container\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">defaultRequest:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100m\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">2Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resourcequota\">ResourceQuota\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A ResourceQuota caps the total of requests, limits, storage and object counts in a namespace, for example total CPU requests, number of pods or persistent volume claims. When a quota covers compute resources, every pod must specify those resources or get them from a LimitRange, otherwise creation is rejected with a message naming the quota.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-quotas-bite\">When quotas bite\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A request that would exceed the quota is refused at admission with &quot;exceeded quota&quot;. The status of the quota shows used and hard values. Quotas apply at creation, so a Deployment hitting a quota shows a ReplicaSet that cannot create pods, not a Pending pod. Look at ReplicaSet events.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe resourcequota -n shop\nkubectl describe rs -n shop | grep -i -A2 &quot;quota&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl describe resourcequota -n shop\nkubectl describe rs -n shop | grep -i -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;quota&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"priority\">Priority\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A PriorityClass assigns an integer priority to pods. The scheduler queues higher priority pods first, and when no node fits can preempt lower priority pods. Use a few classes: system critical for platform components, high for important services, default for most and low for batch that may be displaced. Setting everything to high removes the benefit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharing-a-cluster\">Sharing a cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common model is one namespace per team with a quota sized to the team share, defaults from a LimitRange, and priorities for production over batch. Review quota use against actual use regularly, and add a report so teams see their own consumption.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"lr&gt;rq;rq&gt;adm;adm&gt;pc\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">LimitRange, ResourceQuota and priority classes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lr\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">LimitRange\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rq\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ResourceQuota\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adm\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PriorityClass\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, LimitRange to ResourceQuota; 2, ResourceQuota to Admission; 3, Admission to PriorityClass.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lr\">\u003cdt>LimitRange \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Defaults and bounds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Absent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rq\">\u003cdt>ResourceQuota \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Namespace ceiling.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too small.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adm\">\u003cdt>Admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rejects over quota.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pc\">\u003cdt>PriorityClass \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Order and preemption.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Everything high.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-pods-that-were-never-created\">Incident: Pods that were never created\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment showed zero pods and no Pending ones after a scale up.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy web\nkubectl describe rs web-6b5c | grep -i quota\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web    2/6     2            2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe rs web-6b5c | grep -i quota\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning  FailedCreate  ... forbidden: exceeded quota: team-quota, requested: requests.cpu=500m, used: requests.cpu=3800m, limited: requests.cpu=4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The namespace CPU request quota was nearly used, so new pods were refused when the ReplicaSet tried to create them. There were no Pending pods because pods did not exist. Raising the quota for a justified need, or reducing requests on over-provisioned pods, fixed it. Check ReplicaSet events when pods are missing rather than Pending.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking for Pending pods when the quota refuses creation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting a quota without defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making every workload high priority\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting LimitRange maximums that block legitimate jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing quota against usage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"limitrange","text":"LimitRange"},{"id":"resourcequota","text":"ResourceQuota"},{"id":"when-quotas-bite","text":"When quotas bite"},{"id":"priority","text":"Priority"},{"id":"sharing-a-cluster","text":"Sharing a cluster"},{"id":"incident-pods-that-were-never-created","text":"Incident: Pods that were never created"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Limit ranges","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/limit-range/"},{"t":"Resource quotas","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/"}],"text":"Without guardrails, one team can request all the cluster. Namespace level objects set defaults and ceilings, and priorities say which workloads matter when there is not enough for everyone. What you will learn LimitRange defaults, minimums and maximums ResourceQuota for compute, storage and object counts What happens when a quota blocks a pod PriorityClass and preemption A model for sharing a cluster LimitRange A LimitRange in a namespace supplies default requests and limits for containers that omit them and enforces minimum and maximum values per container or pod. Defaults mean no pod is BestEffort by accident, and maximums stop a single container from asking for an entire node. apiVersion: v1 kind: LimitRange metadata: name: defaults namespace: shop spec: limits: - type: Container default: memory: 512Mi defaultRequest: cpu: 100m memory: 256Mi max: memory: 2Gi ResourceQuota A ResourceQuota caps the total of requests, limits, storage and object counts in a namespace, for example total CPU requests, number of pods or persistent volume claims. When a quota covers compute resources, every pod must specify those resources or get them from a LimitRange, otherwise creation is rejected with a message naming the quota. When quotas bite A request that would exceed the quota is refused at admission with \"exceeded quota\". The status of the quota shows used and hard values. Quotas apply at creation, so a Deployment hitting a quota shows a ReplicaSet that cannot create pods, not a Pending pod. Look at ReplicaSet events. kubectl describe resourcequota -n shop kubectl describe rs -n shop | grep -i -A2 \"quota\" Priority A PriorityClass assigns an integer priority to pods. The scheduler queues higher priority pods first, and when no node fits can preempt lower priority pods. Use a few classes: system critical for platform components, high for important services, default for most and low for batch that may be displaced. Setting everything to high removes the benefit. Sharing a cluster A common model is one namespace per team with a quota sized to the team share, defaults from a LimitRange, and priorities for production over batch. Review quota use against actual use regularly, and add a report so teams see their own consumption. LimitRange, ResourceQuota and priority classes Flow in order: 1, LimitRange to ResourceQuota; 2, ResourceQuota to Admission; 3, Admission to PriorityClass. Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. LimitRange Data store What it does: Defaults and bounds. If it fails or is weak: Absent. ResourceQuota Data store What it does: Namespace ceiling. If it fails or is weak: Too small. Admission Step What it does: Rejects over quota. If it fails or is weak: Unclear errors. PriorityClass Step What it does: Order and preemption. If it fails or is weak: Everything high. Incident: Pods that were never created Incident A deployment showed zero pods and no Pending ones after a scale up. $ kubectl get deploy web NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE web 2/6 2 2 $ kubectl describe rs web-6b5c | grep -i quota Warning FailedCreate ... forbidden: exceeded quota: team-quota, requested: requests.cpu=500m, used: requests.cpu=3800m, limited: requests.cpu=4 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The namespace CPU request quota was nearly used, so new pods were refused when the ReplicaSet tried to create them. There were no Pending pods because pods did not exist. Raising the quota for a justified need, or reducing requests on over-provisioned pods, fixed it. Check ReplicaSet events when pods are missing rather than Pending. Common mistakes Looking for Pending pods when the quota refuses creation Setting a quota without defaults Making every workload high priority Setting LimitRange maximums that block legitimate jobs Never reviewing quota against usage"},"k8s-resources/node-allocatable-pressure-and-eviction":{"html":"\u003cp>A node never gives pods all of its capacity. Some is reserved for the system and some is a safety margin. When the margin is used up, the kubelet evicts pods to protect the node, and knowing its rules explains which pods go first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Capacity, allocatable and the reservations in between\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Eviction signals and thresholds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Soft and hard eviction and the order of victims\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disk pressure and image garbage collection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading node conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"allocatable\">Allocatable\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Capacity is what the machine has. Allocatable is what is left for pods after \u003ccode>kube-reserved\u003c/code> for Kubernetes daemons, \u003ccode>system-reserved\u003c/code> for the operating system and the hard eviction threshold. The scheduler works with allocatable. Without reservations, pods can starve the kubelet and the node may fall over before eviction helps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signals-and-thresholds\">Signals and thresholds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet watches memory.available, nodefs.available, imagefs.available, nodefs.inodesFree and pid availability. Hard thresholds trigger immediate eviction, for example memory.available below 100Mi. Soft thresholds have a grace period and trigger graceful eviction. When a signal passes its threshold, the node reports a pressure condition such as MemoryPressure, and new BestEffort pods are not scheduled there.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-victims\">Choosing victims\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Under memory pressure the kubelet ranks pods by whether they exceed their requests, then by priority, then by how much they exceed. BestEffort and Burstable pods over request go first. Guaranteed pods and those below their request are protected. Evicted pods are marked Failed with reason Evicted, and a controller recreates them elsewhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disk\">Disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Disk pressure comes from container logs, images, writable layers and emptyDir volumes. The kubelet garbage collects unused images and dead containers when disk use crosses a threshold, then evicts pods if that is not enough. Set ephemeral storage requests and limits, rotate logs and avoid writing large files in containers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n '/Conditions/,/Addresses/p'\nkubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Failed | grep Evicted | head\ndf -h /var/lib/containerd /var/lib/kubelet\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Conditions/,/Addresses/p&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Failed | grep Evicted | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/lib/containerd /var/lib/kubelet\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tuning\">Tuning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set reservations from measurement and leave them in place on every node pool. Alert on pressure conditions and eviction counts. Clean up Evicted pods, which stay until deleted. Prefer fixing the workload that exceeds its requests over raising thresholds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cap&gt;alloc;alloc&gt;sig;sig&gt;ev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Node allocatable, pressure and eviction\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alloc\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allocatable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sig\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Eviction signals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Eviction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Capacity to Allocatable; 2, Allocatable to Eviction signals; 3, Eviction signals to Eviction.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cap\">\u003cdt>Capacity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Machine size.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not reserved.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alloc\">\u003cdt>Allocatable \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What pods can use.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too generous.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sig\">\u003cdt>Eviction signals \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Memory, disk, PIDs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Late.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ev\">\u003cdt>Eviction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chosen by QoS and usage.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong pods hit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-disk-that-filled-with-logs\">Incident: A disk that filled with logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node began evicting pods one after another overnight.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe node worker-2 | grep -E &quot;DiskPressure|Evicted&quot;\ndu -sh /var/log/pods | tail -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node worker-2 | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DiskPressure|Evicted&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  DiskPressure     True    KubeletHasDiskPressure   kubelet has disk pressure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -sh /var/log/pods | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">38G     /var/log/pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A chatty application wrote gigabytes of logs without rotation limits, so the node file system crossed its threshold. The kubelet cleaned images, then evicted pods, including healthy ones. Setting log rotation limits, an ephemeral storage limit on the workload and an alert on disk use prevented a repeat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running nodes without kube-reserved and system-reserved\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring disk and inode pressure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raising eviction thresholds instead of fixing the workload\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving Evicted pods in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not setting ephemeral storage limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"allocatable","text":"Allocatable"},{"id":"signals-and-thresholds","text":"Signals and thresholds"},{"id":"choosing-victims","text":"Choosing victims"},{"id":"disk","text":"Disk"},{"id":"tuning","text":"Tuning"},{"id":"incident-a-disk-that-filled-with-logs","text":"Incident: A disk that filled with logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Node-pressure eviction","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/node-pressure-eviction/"},{"t":"Reserve compute resources for system daemons","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/"}],"text":"A node never gives pods all of its capacity. Some is reserved for the system and some is a safety margin. When the margin is used up, the kubelet evicts pods to protect the node, and knowing its rules explains which pods go first. What you will learn Capacity, allocatable and the reservations in between Eviction signals and thresholds Soft and hard eviction and the order of victims Disk pressure and image garbage collection Reading node conditions Allocatable Capacity is what the machine has. Allocatable is what is left for pods after kube-reserved for Kubernetes daemons, system-reserved for the operating system and the hard eviction threshold. The scheduler works with allocatable. Without reservations, pods can starve the kubelet and the node may fall over before eviction helps. Signals and thresholds The kubelet watches memory.available, nodefs.available, imagefs.available, nodefs.inodesFree and pid availability. Hard thresholds trigger immediate eviction, for example memory.available below 100Mi. Soft thresholds have a grace period and trigger graceful eviction. When a signal passes its threshold, the node reports a pressure condition such as MemoryPressure, and new BestEffort pods are not scheduled there. Choosing victims Under memory pressure the kubelet ranks pods by whether they exceed their requests, then by priority, then by how much they exceed. BestEffort and Burstable pods over request go first. Guaranteed pods and those below their request are protected. Evicted pods are marked Failed with reason Evicted, and a controller recreates them elsewhere. Disk Disk pressure comes from container logs, images, writable layers and emptyDir volumes. The kubelet garbage collects unused images and dead containers when disk use crosses a threshold, then evicts pods if that is not enough. Set ephemeral storage requests and limits, rotate logs and avoid writing large files in containers. kubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n &#x27;/Conditions/,/Addresses/p&#x27; kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Failed | grep Evicted | head df -h /var/lib/containerd /var/lib/kubelet Tuning Set reservations from measurement and leave them in place on every node pool. Alert on pressure conditions and eviction counts. Clean up Evicted pods, which stay until deleted. Prefer fixing the workload that exceeds its requests over raising thresholds. Node allocatable, pressure and eviction Flow in order: 1, Capacity to Allocatable; 2, Allocatable to Eviction signals; 3, Eviction signals to Eviction. Data store Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Capacity Data store What it does: Machine size. If it fails or is weak: Not reserved. Allocatable Data store What it does: What pods can use. If it fails or is weak: Too generous. Eviction signals Observability What it does: Memory, disk, PIDs. If it fails or is weak: Late. Eviction Step What it does: Chosen by QoS and usage. If it fails or is weak: Wrong pods hit. Incident: A disk that filled with logs Incident A node began evicting pods one after another overnight. $ kubectl describe node worker-2 | grep -E \"DiskPressure|Evicted\" DiskPressure True KubeletHasDiskPressure kubelet has disk pressure $ du -sh /var/log/pods | tail -1 38G /var/log/pods (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A chatty application wrote gigabytes of logs without rotation limits, so the node file system crossed its threshold. The kubelet cleaned images, then evicted pods, including healthy ones. Setting log rotation limits, an ephemeral storage limit on the workload and an alert on disk use prevented a repeat. Common mistakes Running nodes without kube-reserved and system-reserved Ignoring disk and inode pressure Raising eviction thresholds instead of fixing the workload Leaving Evicted pods in place Not setting ephemeral storage limits"},"k8s-resources/horizontal-pod-autoscaling":{"html":"\u003cp>The HorizontalPodAutoscaler changes the replica count of a workload in response to load. It is simple in concept and easy to misconfigure, because its decisions depend on requests, metrics and timing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The HPA control loop and its formula\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why CPU utilisation depends on requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metrics sources: resource, custom and external\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stabilisation windows and scaling behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common misconfigurations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-loop\">The loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every fifteen seconds by default the HPA controller reads a metric for the target pods, compares it to the target value and computes the desired replicas as the current replicas multiplied by the ratio of current to target metric, rounded up. It then updates the scale subresource of the Deployment or StatefulSet. A tolerance of ten percent avoids small changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import math\ndef desired(current, metric, target):\n    return math.ceil(current * metric / target)\nprint(desired(4, 90, 60))\nprint(desired(10, 30, 60))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> math\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">desired\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">current, metric, target\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> math.ceil(current * metric / target)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(desired(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(desired(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"utilisation-is-relative-to-requests\">Utilisation is relative to requests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a CPU utilisation target such as 60 percent, utilisation means usage divided by the pod request. If a container has no CPU request, the HPA cannot compute it and reports unknown. A request set too low makes utilisation look high and causes over-scaling, and a request set too high hides load. Set requests first, then targets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics-sources\">Metrics sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Resource metrics come from the metrics-server, which must be installed. Custom metrics, such as requests per second per pod, come through an adapter like the Prometheus adapter, and external metrics describe things outside the cluster, such as queue length. KEDA builds on the HPA to scale from event sources, including down to zero. Scaling on a metric that reflects the real bottleneck works better than CPU for many services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"behaviour\">Behaviour\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>behavior\u003c/code> field controls speed. Scale up defaults to fast, doubling or adding four pods per period, while scale down waits for a five minute stabilisation window and removes pods more slowly. You can set policies, for example at most ten percent a minute down, to avoid flapping.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: web\n  minReplicas: 3\n  maxReplicas: 20\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 60\n  behavior:\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">autoscaling/v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HorizontalPodAutoscaler\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metrics:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Resource\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resource:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cpu\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Utilization\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">averageUtilization:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">behavior:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleDown:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stabilizationWindowSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-it\">Check it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get hpa web\nkubectl describe hpa web\nkubectl top pods -l app=web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get hpa web\nkubectl describe hpa web\nkubectl top pods -l app=web\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The describe output shows current metrics, the conditions such as AbleToScale and ScalingActive and recent scaling events. A target of unknown usually means no requests or no metrics-server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ms&gt;hpa;hpa&gt;scale;scale&gt;pods\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Horizontal pod autoscaling\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ms\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">metrics-server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hpa\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">HPA controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"scale\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scale subresource\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pods\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, metrics-server to HPA controller; 2, HPA controller to Scale subresource; 3, Scale subresource to Pods.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ms\">\u003cdt>metrics-server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Resource metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hpa\">\u003cdt>HPA controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Computes replicas.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No requests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"scale\">\u003cdt>Scale subresource \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Updates replicas.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fights manual scaling.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pods\">\u003cdt>Pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive load.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow to start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-autoscaler-that-showed-unknown\">Incident: An autoscaler that showed unknown\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new HPA never changed the replica count despite heavy load.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get hpa web\nkubectl describe hpa web | grep -A2 Conditions\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get hpa web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   REFERENCE        TARGETS         MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web    Deployment/web   &lt;unknown&gt;/60%   3         20        3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe hpa web | grep -A2 Conditions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  ScalingActive  False  FailedGetResourceMetric  the HPA was unable to compute the replica count: failed to get cpu utilization: missing request for cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The container had no CPU request, so utilisation could not be computed and the HPA stayed idle. Adding a CPU request let it compute utilisation, and it scaled up within a minute. If TARGETS shows unknown, check requests and the metrics-server before touching the HPA.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No CPU request on the target\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling on CPU when the bottleneck is elsewhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting minReplicas to one for a critical service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing replicas by hand while an HPA manages them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that new pods take time to become ready\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-loop","text":"The loop"},{"id":"utilisation-is-relative-to-requests","text":"Utilisation is relative to requests"},{"id":"metrics-sources","text":"Metrics sources"},{"id":"behaviour","text":"Behaviour"},{"id":"check-it","text":"Check it"},{"id":"incident-an-autoscaler-that-showed-unknown","text":"Incident: An autoscaler that showed unknown"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Horizontal pod autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"},{"t":"HPA walkthrough","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale-walkthrough/"}],"text":"The HorizontalPodAutoscaler changes the replica count of a workload in response to load. It is simple in concept and easy to misconfigure, because its decisions depend on requests, metrics and timing. What you will learn The HPA control loop and its formula Why CPU utilisation depends on requests Metrics sources: resource, custom and external Stabilisation windows and scaling behaviour Common misconfigurations The loop Every fifteen seconds by default the HPA controller reads a metric for the target pods, compares it to the target value and computes the desired replicas as the current replicas multiplied by the ratio of current to target metric, rounded up. It then updates the scale subresource of the Deployment or StatefulSet. A tolerance of ten percent avoids small changes. import math def desired ( current, metric, target ): return math.ceil(current * metric / target) print (desired( 4 , 90 , 60 )) print (desired( 10 , 30 , 60 )) Utilisation is relative to requests For a CPU utilisation target such as 60 percent, utilisation means usage divided by the pod request. If a container has no CPU request, the HPA cannot compute it and reports unknown. A request set too low makes utilisation look high and causes over-scaling, and a request set too high hides load. Set requests first, then targets. Metrics sources Resource metrics come from the metrics-server, which must be installed. Custom metrics, such as requests per second per pod, come through an adapter like the Prometheus adapter, and external metrics describe things outside the cluster, such as queue length. KEDA builds on the HPA to scale from event sources, including down to zero. Scaling on a metric that reflects the real bottleneck works better than CPU for many services. Behaviour The behavior field controls speed. Scale up defaults to fast, doubling or adding four pods per period, while scale down waits for a five minute stabilisation window and removes pods more slowly. You can set policies, for example at most ten percent a minute down, to avoid flapping. apiVersion: autoscaling/v2 kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler metadata: name: web spec: scaleTargetRef: apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment name: web minReplicas: 3 maxReplicas: 20 metrics: - type: Resource resource: name: cpu target: type: Utilization averageUtilization: 60 behavior: scaleDown: stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300 Check it kubectl get hpa web kubectl describe hpa web kubectl top pods -l app=web The describe output shows current metrics, the conditions such as AbleToScale and ScalingActive and recent scaling events. A target of unknown usually means no requests or no metrics-server. Horizontal pod autoscaling Flow in order: 1, metrics-server to HPA controller; 2, HPA controller to Scale subresource; 3, Scale subresource to Pods. Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. metrics-server Observability What it does: Resource metrics. If it fails or is weak: Missing. HPA controller Step What it does: Computes replicas. If it fails or is weak: No requests. Scale subresource Service What it does: Updates replicas. If it fails or is weak: Fights manual scaling. Pods Service What it does: Receive load. If it fails or is weak: Slow to start. Incident: An autoscaler that showed unknown Incident A new HPA never changed the replica count despite heavy load. $ kubectl get hpa web NAME REFERENCE TARGETS MINPODS MAXPODS REPLICAS web Deployment/web \u003cunknown>/60% 3 20 3 $ kubectl describe hpa web | grep -A2 Conditions ScalingActive False FailedGetResourceMetric the HPA was unable to compute the replica count: failed to get cpu utilization: missing request for cpu (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The container had no CPU request, so utilisation could not be computed and the HPA stayed idle. Adding a CPU request let it compute utilisation, and it scaled up within a minute. If TARGETS shows unknown, check re"},"k8s-resources/vertical-scaling-and-right-sizing":{"html":"\u003cp>Most clusters waste capacity because requests were set once and never revisited. Right-sizing recovers it, and the Vertical Pod Autoscaler helps by recommending or applying better numbers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why requests drift from reality\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VPA modes: Off, Initial, Recreate and Auto\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In-place pod resize\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using recommendations safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Combining with the HPA\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift\">Drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Teams copy requests from examples or set them generously after an incident. Over time many pods request several times what they use, so nodes look full while actual utilisation is low. A cluster at 30 percent real utilisation can need twice the machines it should. The reverse also happens: requests too low give noisy neighbours and OOM kills.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-vertical-pod-autoscaler\">The Vertical Pod Autoscaler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>VPA is an add-on that watches usage history and computes recommended requests for each container. Its updater and admission controller can apply them. In mode Off it only reports recommendations. Initial sets requests when pods are created. Recreate evicts and recreates pods with new values. Auto chooses an update method, and newer versions can use in-place resize where supported.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1\nkind: VerticalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: api\nspec:\n  targetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: api\n  updatePolicy:\n    updateMode: &quot;Off&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">autoscaling.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VerticalPodAutoscaler\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">updatePolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">updateMode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Off&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"in-place-resize\">In-place resize\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Recent Kubernetes versions allow changing container CPU and memory requests and limits on a running pod without recreating it, subject to node capacity and the resize policy of each container. This reduces disruption for stateful and slow starting workloads. Check the feature status for your version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"using-recommendations\">Using recommendations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with mode Off and read the recommendation, which includes lower bound, target and upper bound. Compare to real peaks, apply to a canary and watch. Treat the output as advice, especially for workloads with rare spikes the history did not include. Avoid Auto on single replica services unless an interruption is acceptable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n '/Recommendation/,$p'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Recommendation/,$p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"with-the-hpa\">With the HPA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not let both the HPA and VPA act on the same resource metric, such as CPU, for the same workload, because each reacts to the other. A common arrangement is HPA on CPU or a custom metric, and VPA only recommending for memory, or VPA in Off mode while you set values by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"use&gt;vpa;vpa&gt;apply;apply&gt;save\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Vertical scaling and right-sizing\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"use\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Usage history\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vpa\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Recommender\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apply\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"save\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity saved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Usage history to Recommender; 2, Recommender to Apply; 3, Apply to Capacity saved.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"use\">\u003cdt>Usage history \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Observed demand.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Short window.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vpa\">\u003cdt>Recommender \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Computes targets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missed peaks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apply\">\u003cdt>Apply \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Recreate or resize.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Disruption.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"save\">\u003cdt>Capacity saved \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fewer nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Under-sized.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-recommendation-that-missed-the-monthly-peak\">Incident: A recommendation that missed the monthly peak\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After accepting VPA targets, a service restarted on the first day of the month.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n '/Recommendation/,$p'\nkubectl describe pod api-9d | grep -A2 &quot;Last State&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Recommendation/,$p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Recommendation:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Container Recommendations:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Container Name:  api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Target:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        Cpu:     120m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        Memory:  300Mi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod api-9d | grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Last State&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Last State:  Terminated  Reason: OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The usage history covered two weeks, so the end of month report job was never seen and the recommended memory was too small for it. Adding headroom, a longer window and a separate workload for the monthly job fixed it. Recommendations only know the past they were shown, so apply judgement for rare peaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using Auto mode on single replica critical services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting HPA and VPA fight over CPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting a short history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting requests once and never reviewing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Right-sizing without a rollback plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"drift","text":"Drift"},{"id":"the-vertical-pod-autoscaler","text":"The Vertical Pod Autoscaler"},{"id":"in-place-resize","text":"In-place resize"},{"id":"using-recommendations","text":"Using recommendations"},{"id":"with-the-hpa","text":"With the HPA"},{"id":"incident-a-recommendation-that-missed-the-monthly-peak","text":"Incident: A recommendation that missed the monthly peak"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Vertical pod autoscaler","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/tree/master/vertical-pod-autoscaler"},{"t":"Resize CPU and memory resources assigned to containers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/resize-container-resources/"}],"text":"Most clusters waste capacity because requests were set once and never revisited. Right-sizing recovers it, and the Vertical Pod Autoscaler helps by recommending or applying better numbers. What you will learn Why requests drift from reality VPA modes: Off, Initial, Recreate and Auto In-place pod resize Using recommendations safely Combining with the HPA Drift Teams copy requests from examples or set them generously after an incident. Over time many pods request several times what they use, so nodes look full while actual utilisation is low. A cluster at 30 percent real utilisation can need twice the machines it should. The reverse also happens: requests too low give noisy neighbours and OOM kills. The Vertical Pod Autoscaler VPA is an add-on that watches usage history and computes recommended requests for each container. Its updater and admission controller can apply them. In mode Off it only reports recommendations. Initial sets requests when pods are created. Recreate evicts and recreates pods with new values. Auto chooses an update method, and newer versions can use in-place resize where supported. apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1 kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler metadata: name: api spec: targetRef: apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment name: api updatePolicy: updateMode: \"Off\" In-place resize Recent Kubernetes versions allow changing container CPU and memory requests and limits on a running pod without recreating it, subject to node capacity and the resize policy of each container. This reduces disruption for stateful and slow starting workloads. Check the feature status for your version. Using recommendations Start with mode Off and read the recommendation, which includes lower bound, target and upper bound. Compare to real peaks, apply to a canary and watch. Treat the output as advice, especially for workloads with rare spikes the history did not include. Avoid Auto on single replica services unless an interruption is acceptable. kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n &#x27;/Recommendation/,$p&#x27; With the HPA Do not let both the HPA and VPA act on the same resource metric, such as CPU, for the same workload, because each reacts to the other. A common arrangement is HPA on CPU or a custom metric, and VPA only recommending for memory, or VPA in Off mode while you set values by hand. Vertical scaling and right-sizing Flow in order: 1, Usage history to Recommender; 2, Recommender to Apply; 3, Apply to Capacity saved. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Usage history Observability What it does: Observed demand. If it fails or is weak: Short window. Recommender Step What it does: Computes targets. If it fails or is weak: Missed peaks. Apply Step What it does: Recreate or resize. If it fails or is weak: Disruption. Capacity saved Observability What it does: Fewer nodes. If it fails or is weak: Under-sized. Incident: A recommendation that missed the monthly peak Incident After accepting VPA targets, a service restarted on the first day of the month. $ kubectl describe vpa api | sed -n &#x27;/Recommendation/,$p&#x27; Recommendation: Container Recommendations: Container Name: api Target: Cpu: 120m Memory: 300Mi $ kubectl describe pod api-9d | grep -A2 \"Last State\" Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The usage history covered two weeks, so the end of month report job was never seen and the recommended memory was too small for it. Adding headroom, a longer window and a separate workload for the monthly job fixed it. Recommendations only know the past they were shown, so apply judgement for rare peaks. Common mistakes Using Auto mode on single replica critical services Letting HPA and VPA fight over CPU Trusting a short history Setting requests once and never reviewing Right-sizing without a rollback plan"},"k8s-resources/cluster-autoscaling-karpenter-and-keda":{"html":"\u003cp>Pod autoscaling is only useful if there are nodes to place the new pods. Node autoscaling closes that gap, and event driven autoscaling lets workloads follow queues rather than CPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Cluster Autoscaler and what it reacts to\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How scale down decisions are made and blocked\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Karpenter and just-in-time node provisioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>KEDA and scaling to zero\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost and safety guardrails\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cluster-autoscaler\">Cluster Autoscaler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Cluster Autoscaler watches for pods that are Pending because no node has room. It picks a node group whose template would fit them, simulates the placement and increases that group size. It also finds nodes that have been underused for a period, checks that their pods can run elsewhere, and removes them. It works with node groups defined in the cloud provider, so node shapes are fixed by those groups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blocked-scale-down\">Blocked scale down\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scale down is blocked by pods that cannot be moved: pods with local storage, pods not backed by a controller, restrictive PodDisruptionBudgets, pods with the annotation \u003ccode>cluster-autoscaler.kubernetes.io/safe-to-evict\u003c/code> set to false, and system pods in some configurations. A cluster that never shrinks often has one such pod on every node.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"karpenter\">Karpenter\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Karpenter takes a different approach. It watches Pending pods directly, evaluates their combined requirements, and launches nodes of suitable types and sizes through the cloud API, without predefined node groups. It also consolidates by replacing or removing nodes to reduce cost, and can handle spot interruptions. You define NodePools with limits and constraints such as instance families, zones and capacity types.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keda\">KEDA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>KEDA adds scalers for event sources, such as message queues, streams, databases and schedules, and exposes them as external metrics to the HPA. Its main addition is scaling to zero and back when events arrive. A queue worker with an empty queue then costs nothing, and starts when messages appear.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: worker\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: worker\n  minReplicaCount: 0\n  maxReplicaCount: 30\n  triggers:\n    - type: rabbitmq\n      metadata:\n        queueName: jobs\n        value: &quot;20&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">keda.sh/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ScaledObject\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">worker\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">triggers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rabbitmq\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">queueName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">jobs\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;20&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set maximum node counts or limits so a runaway workload cannot create an unbounded bill. Spread across zones. Keep PodDisruptionBudgets for important workloads so consolidation is safe. Make sure daemons and system add-ons have enough requests. Test scale up latency, since node start time adds to the time before new pods run, and keep a small buffer of spare capacity if that delay is unacceptable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pend&gt;ca;ca&gt;node;node&gt;down\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cluster autoscaling, Karpenter and KEDA\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pend\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pending pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ca\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Autoscaler or Karpenter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"down\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consolidation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pending pods to Autoscaler or Karpenter; 2, Autoscaler or Karpenter to New node; 3, New node to Consolidation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pend\">\u003cdt>Pending pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signal for scale up.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong requests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ca\">\u003cdt>Autoscaler or Karpenter \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adds nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Quota.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>New node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Joins and becomes Ready.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"down\">\u003cdt>Consolidation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Removes idle nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blocked pods.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cluster-that-never-scaled-down\">Incident: The cluster that never scaled down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cluster stayed at its peak node count for weeks after a one-day load test.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | tail -2\nkubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep ip-10-0-3-21 | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">I1114 06:10:01 scale_down.go:  node ip-10-0-3-21 cannot be removed: pod kube-system/metrics-agent is not replicated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep ip-10-0-3-21 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">11\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A single unmanaged pod with local storage sat on the node and blocked its removal, and similar pods blocked others. Moving the agent to a DaemonSet, adding safe-to-evict annotations where valid and reviewing PodDisruptionBudgets let nodes drain and the cluster shrink. If a cluster never scales down, ask the autoscaler why in its log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving unmanaged or non-evictable pods on every node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No maximum node limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autoscaling nodes without requests on pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring node start time in latency targets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using consolidation without PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cluster-autoscaler","text":"Cluster Autoscaler"},{"id":"blocked-scale-down","text":"Blocked scale down"},{"id":"karpenter","text":"Karpenter"},{"id":"keda","text":"KEDA"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"incident-the-cluster-that-never-scaled-down","text":"Incident: The cluster that never scaled down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cluster autoscaler","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/tree/master/cluster-autoscaler"},{"t":"Karpenter","u":"https://github.com/aws/karpenter-provider-aws"}],"text":"Pod autoscaling is only useful if there are nodes to place the new pods. Node autoscaling closes that gap, and event driven autoscaling lets workloads follow queues rather than CPU. What you will learn The Cluster Autoscaler and what it reacts to How scale down decisions are made and blocked Karpenter and just-in-time node provisioning KEDA and scaling to zero Cost and safety guardrails Cluster Autoscaler The Cluster Autoscaler watches for pods that are Pending because no node has room. It picks a node group whose template would fit them, simulates the placement and increases that group size. It also finds nodes that have been underused for a period, checks that their pods can run elsewhere, and removes them. It works with node groups defined in the cloud provider, so node shapes are fixed by those groups. Blocked scale down Scale down is blocked by pods that cannot be moved: pods with local storage, pods not backed by a controller, restrictive PodDisruptionBudgets, pods with the annotation cluster-autoscaler.kubernetes.io/safe-to-evict set to false, and system pods in some configurations. A cluster that never shrinks often has one such pod on every node. Karpenter Karpenter takes a different approach. It watches Pending pods directly, evaluates their combined requirements, and launches nodes of suitable types and sizes through the cloud API, without predefined node groups. It also consolidates by replacing or removing nodes to reduce cost, and can handle spot interruptions. You define NodePools with limits and constraints such as instance families, zones and capacity types. KEDA KEDA adds scalers for event sources, such as message queues, streams, databases and schedules, and exposes them as external metrics to the HPA. Its main addition is scaling to zero and back when events arrive. A queue worker with an empty queue then costs nothing, and starts when messages appear. apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1 kind: ScaledObject metadata: name: worker spec: scaleTargetRef: name: worker minReplicaCount: 0 maxReplicaCount: 30 triggers: - type: rabbitmq metadata: queueName: jobs value: \"20\" Guardrails Set maximum node counts or limits so a runaway workload cannot create an unbounded bill. Spread across zones. Keep PodDisruptionBudgets for important workloads so consolidation is safe. Make sure daemons and system add-ons have enough requests. Test scale up latency, since node start time adds to the time before new pods run, and keep a small buffer of spare capacity if that delay is unacceptable. Cluster autoscaling, Karpenter and KEDA Flow in order: 1, Pending pods to Autoscaler or Karpenter; 2, Autoscaler or Karpenter to New node; 3, New node to Consolidation. Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pending pods Observability What it does: Signal for scale up. If it fails or is weak: Wrong requests. Autoscaler or Karpenter Step What it does: Adds nodes. If it fails or is weak: Quota. New node Service What it does: Joins and becomes Ready. If it fails or is weak: Slow start. Consolidation Step What it does: Removes idle nodes. If it fails or is weak: Blocked pods. Incident: The cluster that never scaled down Incident A cluster stayed at its peak node count for weeks after a one-day load test. $ kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | tail -2 I1114 06:10:01 scale_down.go: node ip-10-0-3-21 cannot be removed: pod kube-system/metrics-agent is not replicated $ kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep ip-10-0-3-21 | wc -l 11 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A single unmanaged pod with local storage sat on the node and blocked its removal, and similar pods blocked others. Moving the agent to a DaemonSet, adding safe-to-evict annotations where valid and reviewing PodDisruptionBudgets let nodes drain and the cluster shrink. If a cluster never scales down, ask the autoscaler why in its log. Common mistakes Leaving unm"},"k8s-resources/disruption-budgets-topology-spread-and-capacity-planning":{"html":"\u003cp>Autoscaling and upgrades both move pods around. Disruption budgets and spread constraints say how much movement a workload can take, and capacity planning makes sure there is room to move into.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>PodDisruptionBudgets: minAvailable and maxUnavailable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Voluntary versus involuntary disruption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Topology spread constraints and anti-affinity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planning N plus one capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A review checklist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disruptions\">Disruptions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Voluntary disruptions are actions by operators and tools: drains, upgrades, consolidation and eviction API calls. Involuntary ones are hardware failures, kernel panics and node loss. PodDisruptionBudgets apply to voluntary evictions made through the eviction API, so they do not stop a node crashing, but they do slow a drain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"poddisruptionbudgets\">PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A PDB selects pods and declares \u003ccode>minAvailable\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>maxUnavailable\u003c/code>, as a number or a percentage. The eviction API refuses an eviction that would break it. Choose values that match the workload: a three replica service might allow one unavailable. A budget of zero allowed disruptions blocks all drains, which is the cause of many stuck upgrades.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: policy/v1\nkind: PodDisruptionBudget\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  maxUnavailable: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">policy/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PodDisruptionBudget\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxUnavailable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"spreading\">Spreading\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Topology spread constraints tell the scheduler to keep replicas evenly spread over zones or nodes, within a maximum skew. They are clearer than pod anti-affinity for most uses. Combine them with several replicas so that the loss of a zone or node leaves enough capacity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"topologySpreadConstraints:\n  - maxSkew: 1\n    topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n    whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule\n    labelSelector:\n      matchLabels:\n        app: web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">topologySpreadConstraints:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxSkew:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">topologyKey:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">topology.kubernetes.io/zone\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">whenUnsatisfiable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DoNotSchedule\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labelSelector:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-planning\">Capacity planning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plan so that the cluster survives the loss of the largest failure domain you care about. With three zones, that means the remaining two can carry the load, so run each zone at no more than about two thirds of its capacity. Add headroom for the autoscaler delay and for upgrades, which need spare room to drain into. Review growth trends monthly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"zones = 3\nload = 100.0\ncapacity_per_zone = load / (zones - 1) * 1.15\nprint(&quot;capacity per zone:&quot;, round(capacity_per_zone, 1))\nprint(&quot;total:&quot;, round(capacity_per_zone * zones, 1))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">zones = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\nload = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100.0\u003c/span>\ncapacity_per_zone = load / (zones - \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>) * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.15\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;capacity per zone:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(capacity_per_zone, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;total:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(capacity_per_zone * zones, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checklist\">Checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each production workload: at least two or three replicas, a PDB that allows one disruption, a spread constraint over zones, requests that reflect reality, a readiness probe and a graceful shutdown. For the cluster: node headroom, autoscaler limits, and a tested drain of one node and one zone.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pdb&gt;spread;spread&gt;cap;cap&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Disruption budgets, topology spread and capacity planning\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pdb\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PodDisruptionBudget\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spread\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Topology spread\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity headroom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drain test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, PodDisruptionBudget to Topology spread; 2, Topology spread to Capacity headroom; 3, Capacity headroom to Drain test.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pdb\">\u003cdt>PodDisruptionBudget \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limits evictions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zero allowed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spread\">\u003cdt>Topology spread \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Even placement.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One zone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cap\">\u003cdt>Capacity headroom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Room to move.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Full nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Drain test \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Proves it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never run.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-zone-loss-that-took-the-service-down\">Incident: A zone loss that took the service down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A single zone failed and a service with six replicas went fully unavailable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide --no-headers | awk '{print $7}' | sort | uniq -c\nkubectl get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone | grep node-a1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide --no-headers | awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $7}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      6 node-a1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone | grep node-a1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">node-a1   Ready   zone-a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All six replicas had been scheduled to one node in one zone because no spread constraint was set and that node had the most free resources. When the zone failed, nothing was left. A zone spread constraint, a PDB and a capacity plan with headroom in the other zones would have kept the service up. Replica count is not availability unless the replicas are spread.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Several replicas on one node or zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A PDB with no allowed disruptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running every zone at high utilisation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No test of a drain or a zone loss\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing voluntary and involuntary disruption\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"disruptions","text":"Disruptions"},{"id":"poddisruptionbudgets","text":"PodDisruptionBudgets"},{"id":"spreading","text":"Spreading"},{"id":"capacity-planning","text":"Capacity planning"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"},{"id":"incident-a-zone-loss-that-took-the-service-down","text":"Incident: A zone loss that took the service down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Disruptions","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/disruptions/"},{"t":"Pod topology spread constraints","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/topology-spread-constraints/"}],"text":"Autoscaling and upgrades both move pods around. Disruption budgets and spread constraints say how much movement a workload can take, and capacity planning makes sure there is room to move into. What you will learn PodDisruptionBudgets: minAvailable and maxUnavailable Voluntary versus involuntary disruption Topology spread constraints and anti-affinity Planning N plus one capacity A review checklist Disruptions Voluntary disruptions are actions by operators and tools: drains, upgrades, consolidation and eviction API calls. Involuntary ones are hardware failures, kernel panics and node loss. PodDisruptionBudgets apply to voluntary evictions made through the eviction API, so they do not stop a node crashing, but they do slow a drain. PodDisruptionBudgets A PDB selects pods and declares minAvailable or maxUnavailable , as a number or a percentage. The eviction API refuses an eviction that would break it. Choose values that match the workload: a three replica service might allow one unavailable. A budget of zero allowed disruptions blocks all drains, which is the cause of many stuck upgrades. apiVersion: policy/v1 kind: PodDisruptionBudget metadata: name: web spec: maxUnavailable: 1 selector: matchLabels: app: web Spreading Topology spread constraints tell the scheduler to keep replicas evenly spread over zones or nodes, within a maximum skew. They are clearer than pod anti-affinity for most uses. Combine them with several replicas so that the loss of a zone or node leaves enough capacity. topologySpreadConstraints: - maxSkew: 1 topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule labelSelector: matchLabels: app: web Capacity planning Plan so that the cluster survives the loss of the largest failure domain you care about. With three zones, that means the remaining two can carry the load, so run each zone at no more than about two thirds of its capacity. Add headroom for the autoscaler delay and for upgrades, which need spare room to drain into. Review growth trends monthly. zones = 3 load = 100.0 capacity_per_zone = load / (zones - 1 ) * 1.15 print ( \"capacity per zone:\" , round (capacity_per_zone, 1 )) print ( \"total:\" , round (capacity_per_zone * zones, 1 )) Checklist For each production workload: at least two or three replicas, a PDB that allows one disruption, a spread constraint over zones, requests that reflect reality, a readiness probe and a graceful shutdown. For the cluster: node headroom, autoscaler limits, and a tested drain of one node and one zone. Disruption budgets, topology spread and capacity planning Flow in order: 1, PodDisruptionBudget to Topology spread; 2, Topology spread to Capacity headroom; 3, Capacity headroom to Drain test. Security Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. PodDisruptionBudget Security What it does: Limits evictions. If it fails or is weak: Zero allowed. Topology spread Step What it does: Even placement. If it fails or is weak: One zone. Capacity headroom Data store What it does: Room to move. If it fails or is weak: Full nodes. Drain test Observability What it does: Proves it. If it fails or is weak: Never run. Incident: A zone loss that took the service down Incident A single zone failed and a service with six replicas went fully unavailable. $ kubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide --no-headers | awk &#x27;{print $7}&#x27; | sort | uniq -c 6 node-a1 $ kubectl get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone | grep node-a1 node-a1 Ready zone-a (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) All six replicas had been scheduled to one node in one zone because no spread constraint was set and that node had the most free resources. When the zone failed, nothing was left. A zone spread constraint, a PDB and a capacity plan with headroom in the other zones would have kept the service up. Replica count is not availability unless the replicas are spread. Common mistakes Sever"}});